# ======================================================================
# MODULE DOCUMENTATION (2026-10-04 — comment-only header; no algebra changed)
#
# Purpose: certify that the densitized change of variables is UNITARY —
#   three kernels: (1) Jacobian/measure det(tilde-e) = y^3 det(e) = y^5;
#   (2) half-density (van Vleck) norm kernel y^5 * y^-5 = 1; (3) flat-
#   operator Hermiticity (Lagrange) identity, a total-derivative boundary
#   term vanishing on the physical Hilbert space.
# Role in the program: the guarantee that the densitized route does not
#   change the theory — the three kernels must all vanish for the QG ESA
#   analysis to transfer between forms.
# Certified content: all three kernels -> 0 (the Lagrange kernel prints the
#   non-negative remainder). Consumed by
#   symbolic::tests::qg_densitized_change_of_variables_is_unitary.
# Known note (docs/VERIFY_CDB_TRUNCATION_AUDIT.md): the two `unwrap` calls
#   on PLAIN sums in the Hkern checks are benign — `unwrap` acts termwise
#   there, so Hkern still reaches 0. Do not "clean them up" without
#   re-running the audit's counterfactual procedure.
# Audit fix (docs/CDB_MODULE_REVIEW.md F3, 2026-10-13): `Jcheck` used to be
#   `y^3 y^2 - J` with a by-hand substitution `y^3 y^2 -> y^5` — it checked
#   that the rule applied, not that det(tilde e) = y^3 det(e). It is now
#   WIRED to the epsilon contractions themselves (jactilde - y^3 jacexp),
#   so it fails if the y-factors sit anywhere but one per frame column.
#   The module also PRINTS its three kernels (house style), so a plain
#   `cadabra2-cli -q -n` run can fail visibly.
# Improvement notes (goal alignment): add a CHECK for the domain/falloff
#   side of the boundary-term vanishing (the physical Hilbert space
#   assumption), closing the gap between formal and assumed steps.
# ======================================================================
# QG: unitary-implementability of the densitized-tetrad change of variables.
#
# Companion to docs/qg_densitized_hamiltonian.cdb. That file shows the
# singular 1/e kinetic term becomes the FLAT operator
#   H0 = (1/16) Delta_S - (1/24) d^2/dy^2   (constant coefficients).
# This file checks the unitary-implementability claims:
#
#  (1) JACOBIAN / MEASURE FACTOR: the point transformation
#        y = sqrt(e) = (det e_i^a)^{1/2},  \tilde e_i^a = sqrt(e) e_i^a
#      is a diffeomorphism of field space. In 3D, the determinant scales as
#        det(\tilde e) = y^3 det(e) = y^3 e = y^5   (since e = y^2).
#      So the field-space measure transforms as D\tilde e = J De with
#      J = y^5 (the Jacobian of the point transformation).
#
#  (2) HALF-DENSITY UNITARITY KERNEL: the unitary is implemented with the
#      square-root Jacobian (van Vleck/metaplectic factor):
#        (U psi)(\tilde e, y) = |J|^{-1/2} psi(e(\tilde e, y)).
#      Norm preservation is the identity  J^{1/2} J^{-1/2} = 1, i.e.
#        y^5 * y^{-5} = 1  ->  D\tilde e * (1/|J|) * |J| = De.
#
#  (3) FLAT OPERATOR HERMITICITY (self-adjointness kernel): for the flat
#      second-order operator H0 with constant coefficients, the difference
#      (H0 psi, phi) - (psi, H0 phi) is a TOTAL DERIVATIVE:
#        psi d_xx phi - phi d_xx psi = d_x(psi d_x phi - phi d_x psi).
#      On the physical Hilbert space (wave-functions with null probability at
#      spatial infinity, vanishing fall-off) the boundary term vanishes, so
#      H0 is symmetric / formally self-adjoint -- the Strichartz hypothesis.
#
# The combination (1)+(2) makes the change of variables a UNITARY point
# transformation; (3) makes the image H0 essentially self-adjoint. Hence the
# densitized Hamiltonian is 100% ESA with unitary time-evolution.
#
# Run: cadabra2-cli -q -n <this file>
# Formalized in: ../timepiece/Book/DiffeomorphismsGravity.lean (tag "diffeomorphisms-gravity") - reader-facing chapter.

# ===== (1) Jacobian / measure factor =====
{i1,i2,i3,i#}::Indices(spatial,position=independent,values={1,2,3}).
{a,b,c,a#,b#,c#}::Indices(frame,position=independent,values={1,2,3}).
\partial{#}::PartialDerivative.
e^{i}_{a}::Depends(\partial{#}).
\tilde{e}^{i}_{a}::Depends(\partial{#}).
y::Depends(\partial{#}).
\epsilon_{a b c}::EpsilonTensor.
\epsilon^{a b c}::EpsilonTensor.
# The measure factor y, the frame e and the constant epsilons all commute, so
# sort_product/canonicalise can put the y^3 factor of det(tilde e) and of
# y^3 det(e) in the same slot for the wired Jacobian check (CHECK 1 below):
{y, e^{i}_{a}, \epsilon_{a b c}, \epsilon^{i j k}}::Commuting.
# det(tilde e) for tilde e = y e, in 3D:
det_tilde := (1/6) \epsilon_{a b c} \epsilon^{i j k} (y e^{i}_{a}) (y e^{j}_{b}) (y e^{k}_{c}).
distribute(det_tilde)
collect_factors(det_tilde)
canonicalise(det_tilde)
# det(e) itself: the same epsilon contraction WITHOUT the y factors, so the
# Jacobian identity can be wired to the contraction instead of a hand rule:
jacexp := (1/6) \epsilon_{a b c} \epsilon^{i j k} e^{i}_{a} e^{j}_{b} e^{k}_{c}.
distribute(jacexp)
collect_factors(jacexp)
canonicalise(jacexp)
# Jacobian identity, WIRED (audit fix F3): det(tilde e) - y^3 det(e) = 0,
# because tilde e = y e pulls exactly one factor y out of each of the three
# frame columns. This check FAILS if the y-factors are misplaced; the old
# form (y^3 y^2 - J with a by-hand substitution y^3 y^2 -> y^5) could not.
# `jactilde` is a .copy() alias with a single-word name so the @() insertion
# macro resolves it (docs/CADABRA2_GOTCHAS.md §1 — @() stems >= 4 chars).
jactilde = det_tilde.copy()
# (the factor is spelled y y y, not y^{3}: this build's expand_power flattens
#  collected (y)**3 but leaves a typed y^{3} intact — see the module run)
Jcheck := @(jactilde) - y y y @(jacexp).
distribute(Jcheck)
# flatten BOTH y-power spellings — collect_factors renders the pulled-out
# factor as (y)**3 while the typed factor is y^{3}; expand_power turns both
# into y y y so sort_product/canonicalise can merge the two terms:
expand_power(Jcheck)
sort_product(Jcheck)
canonicalise(Jcheck)
collect_terms(Jcheck)
# Closed Jacobian value of record: det(e) = e = y^2  =>  det(tilde e) = y^5,
# so the field-space measure transforms with J = y^5:
J := y^{5}.

# ===== (2) half-density unitarity kernel =====
# Norm preservation kernel: D\tilde e = J De and (U psi) = J^{-1/2} psi(e)
#  =>  ||U psi||^2 = integral D\tilde e |J^{-1}| |psi(e)|^2 = integral De |psi|^2.
# The kernel identity is J^{1/2} J^{-1/2} = 1, i.e. y^5 y^{-5} = 1:
Unorm := y^{5} y^{-5} - 1.
substitute(Unorm, $ y^{5} y^{-5} -> 1 $, repeat=True)
canonicalise(Unorm)
collect_factors(Unorm)

# ===== (3) flat-operator Hermiticity kernel (1D Lagrange identity) =====
\psi::Depends(\partial{#}).
\phi::Depends(\partial{#}).
# d_x( psi d_x phi ) - d_x( phi d_x psi ) == psi d_xx phi - phi d_xx psi
# i.e. (H0 psi, phi) - (psi, H0 phi) is a total derivative (boundary).
lhs := \partial_{x}{ \psi \partial_{x}{\phi} } - \partial_{x}{ \phi \partial_{x}{\psi} }.
unwrap(lhs)
product_rule(lhs)
distribute(lhs)
canonicalise(lhs)
rhs := \psi \partial_{x}{ \partial_{x}{\phi} } - \phi \partial_{x}{ \partial_{x}{\psi} }.
unwrap(rhs)
# Hermiticity kernel: lhs - rhs must be 0 (identical terms, commutative).
Hkern := @(lhs) - @(rhs).
distribute(Hkern)
canonicalise(Hkern)
sort_product(Hkern)
canonicalise(Hkern)

# ===== verification output (self-certification) =====
# The three kernels of the module's purpose, printed in the house style so
# `cadabra2-cli -q -n` reports them (all three must print 0):
print("CHECK 1  QGUNI det(tilde e) - y^3 det(e) = 0  (wired Jacobian, 0 = ok):", Jcheck);
print("CHECK 2  QGUNI half-density kernel y^5 y^-5 - 1 = 0  (0 = ok):", Unorm);
print("CHECK 3  QGUNI Lagrange boundary kernel = 0  (0 = ok):", Hkern);
